## 1. Push the repo to GitHub

```bash
gh repo create {{ github_owner }}/{{ github_repo }} --private --source . --push
```

## 1b. Develop in the sandbox (recommended for AI-assisted work)

Open the repo in the **`.devcontainer/`** (VS Code: *Reopen in Container*). It runs a **default-deny
egress firewall** (`.devcontainer/init-firewall.sh`) so code the agent writes/runs can only reach an
allow-list (GitHub, package registries, sigstore) — a buggy or prompt-injected dependency can't
exfiltrate. The firewall verifies itself at start and refuses to come up open (fail-closed). Widen
the allow-list in that script for hosts your project legitimately needs. **Never put production
credentials in the dev environment** — use throwaway/dev values in `.env`.

{% if include_claude_hooks %}## 1c. Local agent ergonomics — Claude Code hooks

`.claude/settings.json` sets the permission model (routine work runs unprompted, policy and
release actions ask, secrets and the agent's own settings are denied) and wires five **session
hooks** (they run automatically in Claude Code — no install step):

- **Auto-format** — after the agent edits a `backend/` Python file, `ruff --fix` + `black` run on it
  (uses `backend/.venv`; skips silently if absent). Fails **open**.
- **Footgun blocker** — a `Bash` call that pushes directly to `main`, force-pushes anything (the
  flag can sit anywhere), uses `--no-verify` or `--admin`, or `rm -rf`s a dangerous root is
  blocked. Applying one of your override labels (`guardrail-change` and the rest) or removing
  `release-blocker` is blocked in every spelling. Deleting a remote ref, publishing a tag and any
  mutating `gh api` call ask you.
- **Confinement** — no shell command may change anything outside the project, or anything under
  `.claude/`. Reading elsewhere is fine.
- **Secret-write blocker** — writing a real `.env`, a `*.pem`/`*.key`, or a file containing a PRIVATE
  KEY block is blocked.
- **Approved scope** — prompts on the core paths unless `.claude/approved-scope.json` holds an
  active scope you approved that covers them.

> **These are ergonomics, not security — with one exception.** They shift the existing guards left
> for a tighter loop and run only in Claude Code, on your machine. **CI, the branch ruleset, and
> pre-commit remain the fail-closed backstop.** The exception is your labels: CI checks that
> `guardrail-change` is on a PR, not who put it there, and while the agent uses your GitHub login
> the forge cannot tell you apart. The footgun blocker is what keeps that approval yours. The
> complete fix is a separate GitHub account for the agent — the growth trigger in `docs/PLAN.md`.

**Once per machine**, ignore personal agent settings in every repository, so none can commit them
by accident (this project's `.gitignore` already does, for this project):

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.config/git && printf '%s\n' '**/.claude/settings.local.json' >> ~/.config/git/ignore
```

**Check the policy is live — don't assume it.** A rule can be well-formed, tested, and still not
hold (a later settings file, a bypass mode, an untrusted workspace that ignores project allow
rules). In a fresh Claude Code session in this project, ask the agent to do each of these:

| Ask it to… | Expected |
|---|---|
| edit a file under `backend/` | no prompt |
| run `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/git/refs/tags/none -X DELETE` | a prompt (decline it) |
| run `git push origin <some-tag> --force` | refused by the hook |
| run `gh pr edit 1 --add-label guardrail-change` | refused by the hook |
| run `echo '{}' > .claude/settings.local.json` | refused by the hook |
| edit `.claude/settings.local.json` | refused by a deny rule |

If a "prompt" or "refused" row runs, check the session's permission mode and whether a user or
managed settings file overrides the project's before trusting anything else in it.

To disable, delete `.claude/settings.json` (or generate with `include_claude_hooks=false`). The
scripts live in `.claude/hooks/`.

